{"name":"Raw Peanut","hex":"#e4bc8c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"raw","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Rawang."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Not cooked. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Subsisting on, or pertaining to, a diet of raw food."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/raw"},{"term":"peanut","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A legume resembling a nut, the fruit of the plant Arachis hypogaea, native to South America.","Synonym of countneck (“very small hard clam”)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pull on somebody's tie as a prank, causing the knot to tighten."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peanut"}],"entries":[{"title":"Peanut","blocks":[{"text":"The peanut, also known as the groundnut, goober, goober pea, pindar or monkey nut (UK), is a legume crop grown mainly for its edible seeds, contained in underground pods. It is widely grown in the tropics and subtropics by small and large commercial producers, both as a grain legume and as an oil crop. Underground fruiting (geocarpy) is atypical among legumes, which led botanist Carl Linnaeus to name the species hypogaea, from Greek 'under the earth'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Arachis_hypogaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-163.jpg/330px-Arachis_hypogaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-163.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peanut"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Arachis_hypogaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-163.jpg/500px-Arachis_hypogaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-163.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Arachis_hypogaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen